<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gondar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gonder</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gondar is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tana">Lake Tana</a>, founded as the royal capital by Emperor Fasilides around 1636. It remained the seat of the Ethiopian emperors until the mid-19th century, and its walled royal enclosure, Fasil Ghebbi, with castles and churches, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. In November 1941 it was the last stronghold of the Italian forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> to surrender.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gondar.jpg" alt="Gondar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giustino · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gonder_from_the_Goha_hotel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gondar lies in the North Gondar Zone of the Amhara Region, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tana">Lake Tana</a> on the Lesser Angereb River and south-west of the Simien Mountains. The name is first recorded under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon I</a> as that of a regiment stationed, probably in Wegera, to guard trade routes and control a restive population. In 1636 Emperor Fasilides chose the place as his katama, or royal camp, in the midst of Kémant country. It stood where caravans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> converged, which brought trading benefits to the court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Fasilides ordered the building of Fasil Ghebbi, or Fasilides Castle, and founded five other churches. In 1648 a Yemeni ambassador, Hassan ibn Ahmad al-Haymi, described the palace as a great house of stone and lime. By Fasilides' death in 1667 the city was so well established that his successor Yohannes I did not seek a new capital, and in 1669-1670 Yohannes ordered Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to live apart from Christians in quarters of their own. Iyasu I (1682-1706) added a castle and several churches, and under him the population was estimated to exceed 60,000. The French physician Charles Poncet was the first European observer to describe the city.</p><p>After the death of Iyasu I in 1706 the city declined. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> influence grew, and the warlord Ras Mikael Sehul became master of Gondar, killing Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyoas-i">Iyoas I</a> in May 1767. The empire then entered the period of weak central rule known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zemene-mesafint">Zemene Mesafint</a>. Early in the 19th century soldiers plundered the city repeatedly, and in 1864 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Tewodros II</a> ordered it violently sacked, calling it a town of priests who did not love him. Mahdist forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> burned its churches in January 1888 and attacked again the following June. The population, over 60,000 in the 18th century, was said to have shrunk to 8,000 in the 1870s.</p><p>Italian forces occupied Gondar without a fight on 1 April 1936, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-italo-ethiopian-war">Second Italo-Ethiopian War</a>. They laid out a piazza, new roads and buildings in the modernist style of the 1930s, and about 2,000 Europeans settled there within two years. British and Italian forces fought in the city from 13 to 17 November 1941.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gondar has about 44 churches, more than any other Ethiopian settlement for many years, and is a centre of learning of the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church. Its region is the homeland of most Ethiopian Jews. Fasil Ghebbi holds Fasilides' castle, Iyasu's palace, Dawit's Hall, Empress Mentewab's castle and three churches. Nearby are Fasilides' Bath, blessed each year in a ceremony, and the Debre Berhan Selassie Church. The University of Gondar includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s main faculty of medicine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gondar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In January 1991 the Ethiopian People's Revolutionary Democratic Front took <b>Gondar</b>, Bahir Dar and Dessie, while the EPLF held all Eritrea except Asmara and Assab.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Ras Mikael Sehul had given him a copy as he left <b>Gondar</b>, and a description of its contents appeared in the third edition of 1813; the documents were later given to the Bodleian Library (shelfmark Bruce 87).</blockquote><a href="/work/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Haile Selassie re-entered Addis Ababa on 5 May 1941, and the last Italian forces surrendered at <b>Gondar</b> in November.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-east-africa">Italian East Africa</a></li><li><blockquote>While the <b>Gondar</b> emperors weakened, Negasi Krestos, a Shewan noble claiming Solomonic descent, established an autonomous lordship in northern Shewa around 1682.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-shewa">Kingdom of Shewa</a></li><li><blockquote>In the mid-18th century, as the <b>Gondar</b> emperors lost power, Ras Mikael Sehul built up a strong regional state in Tigray, the old heartland of Aksum, controlling the trade route to Massawa.</blockquote><a href="/state/tigray">Tigray</a></li></ul></section>
